 ],
 "literal": "My mother and your mother, who are they to each other? / My father and your father, in what relationship are they relatives? / I and you, in what way did we know each other? / Like rainwater on red soil, / our loving hearts have mingled on their own.",
 "translation": "My mother and your mother, what are they to each other? My father and your father, how are they related? And you and I, how did we ever know each other? Yet, like rainwater pouring on red soil, our loving hearts have mingled on their own.",
 "modern": "என் தாயும் உன் தாயும் ஒருவருக்கொருவர் யார்? என் தந்தையும் உன் தந்தையும் என்ன உறவினர்கள்? நானும் நீயும் எவ்விதம் ஒருவரை ஒருவர் அறிந்தோம்? செம்மண் நிலத்தில் பெய்த மழைநீர் போல, அன்பு கொண்ட நம் நெஞ்சங்கள் தாமே ஒன்றோடொன்று கலந்துவிட்டன.",
 "explanation": "This poem is spoken by the hero to the heroine after their secret union to reassure her of the permanence of their love. He points out that they had no prior family connections, nor did they even know each other before. Yet, their hearts have merged completely and naturally, just like rainwater falling on red soil. Once rainwater mixes with red earth, it takes on the color and character of the soil, and the two can never be separated again. This beautiful metaphor from the Kuṟiñci landscape perfectly captures the absolute and irreversible union of their souls. The key words are 'செம்புலப் பெயனீர்' (rainwater on red soil) and 'அன்புடை நெஞ்சம்' (loving hearts).",
